In a paper titled \Finding the Largest Empty Convex Polygon with a Given Number of Vertices\ by T. Biedl et al., they present an O(n^3 k) algorithm for this problem. The approach involves dynamic programming to build convex polygons incrementally.
The algorithm works as follows:
Sort all the points in lex order (x then y).
For each point p_i (sorted), this point is considered as the last point in the convex polygon.
For each point p_j (j < i), the line segment p_j to p_i is considered as the last edge of the polygon.
For each pair (p_j, p_i), and for each k (number of vertices), a DP table is maintained to track the maximum area for polygons ending with edge p_j-p_i and having k vertices.
The transition is for each p_l (l < j) where the triangle p_l-p_j-p_i forms a convex turn (i.e., the turn from p_l to p_j to p_i is a left turn), and the polygon formed by p_l, p_j, p_i contains no points inside.
The DP recurrence is:
dp[j][i][k] = max over l < j ( dp[l][j][k-1] + area(p_l, p_j, p_i) )
The initial condition is for k=3, where the area is the area of the triangle p_l, p_j, p_i.
The algorithm requires O(n^3 k) time and O(n^2 k) space.
